        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Lubricating a walking pad is the same idea as lubricating any treadmill: put a small
          amount of 100% silicone between the underside of the belt and the deck, then spread it by
          walking. The difference is access. A full treadmill has a frame and enough clearance to
          lift the belt edge and reach under it; a walking pad sits a few inches off the floor, the
          belt is usually tensioned tight against a short deck, and there is nothing to lean on
          while you work. The method below adapts the standard approach for that — loosening the
          rear roller slightly so the belt edge lifts, applying silicone under the centre of the
          deck, then restoring the tension and the tracking. It takes about fifteen minutes and the
          tools usually come in the box.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. How Do You Lubricate a Walking Pad? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Unplug the pad, loosen the two rear roller bolts by the same number of turns,
            lift each edge of the belt in turn and apply 100% silicone treadmill lubricant to the
            deck under the centre of the belt, then restore the bolts to their original position,
            walk at a low speed for a few minutes to spread it, and re-centre the belt if it has
            drifted.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            That is the whole job. The details that make it go well are counting the turns when you
            loosen the bolts so you can put the tension back exactly, applying silicone to the middle
            of the deck where your feet land rather than near the ends, using a modest amount rather
            than a lot, and checking the belt runs centred before you walk on it for any length of
            time.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three things to check first. Read the manual, because some pads have a pre-waxed or
            maintenance-free deck and should not be lubricated at all. Use only 100% silicone
            lubricant made for treadmills — never WD-40, household oil or general-purpose sprays.
            And unplug at the wall before you touch anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you own a full-size treadmill as well, the general method is in our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt lubrication</a>.
            This page covers what is different on a walking pad.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="does-it-need-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Does Your Walking Pad Need Lubricating at All?</h2>
<p>
            Most do, but not all, and the manual is the only reliable way to know. Walking pads fall
            into roughly three groups.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Pads that need periodic lubrication.</strong> The common case. The manual
            gives an interval, a method and often a quantity, and the box usually includes a small
            bottle of silicone and the hex key for the rear bolts. Follow the manual's schedule.</li>
            <li><strong>Pads with a pre-waxed or maintenance-free deck.</strong> The deck or belt
            backing is treated at the factory so that it should not need adding to, at least for a
            long period. Adding silicone to these can make the belt slip and may conflict with the
            warranty terms. If the manual says not to lubricate, do not.</li>
            <li><strong>Pads whose manual says little.</strong> Some budget pads come with sparse
            instructions. If the manual is silent, check the seller's or brand's support pages, or
            ask them directly, before adding anything. If a bottle of silicone came in the box, that
            is a strong hint that lubrication is expected.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Whatever the group, the deck still wears. Even a maintenance-free deck can dry out
            eventually, and the signs listed further down this page apply to every pad. If a pad
            described as maintenance-free starts showing them, contact the seller before
            lubricating it yourself, particularly while it is under warranty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-you-need" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Do You Need to Lubricate a Walking Pad?</h2>
<p>
            Very little, and most of it usually comes with the pad.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>100% silicone treadmill lubricant.</strong> Often supplied with the pad in a
            small squeeze bottle. If you need more, buy one labelled as 100% silicone and intended
            for treadmills. A bottle with a long nozzle or applicator tube makes reaching under a
            tight belt much easier.</li>
            <li><strong>The hex key for the rear roller bolts.</strong> Usually supplied. If not,
            check the size in the manual; a standard set of hex keys will cover it.</li>
            <li><strong>A lint-free cloth.</strong> For wiping the deck edges and any excess.</li>
            <li><strong>A torch or phone light.</strong> To see under the belt edge.</li>
            <li><strong>A marker or a strip of tape.</strong> To mark the starting position of each
            bolt head, which makes it easier to restore the tension.</li>
            <li><strong>Paper and pen, or your phone.</strong> To write down the number of turns
            you made on each side. It sounds excessive; it is the step that saves you from a
            tracking problem afterwards.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If the lubricant that came with the pad has run out, check the label of any replacement
            for two things: that it says 100% silicone, and that it is intended for treadmills. A
            bottle lasts a long time at walking pad quantities, so there is no need to buy a large
            one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-not-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What Should You Not Use on a Walking Pad?</h2>
<p>
            Anything that is not 100% silicone treadmill lubricant, unless your manual specifies a
            different product. The deck and the underside of the belt are designed to run against a
            silicone film, and other products can damage them.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>WD-40.</strong> It is a water-displacing solvent, not a deck lubricant, and
            on a treadmill deck it does more harm than good. Our page on
            <a href="/can-you-use-wd40-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">using WD-40 on a treadmill</a>
            explains exactly why, and what to do if you have already used it.</li>
            <li><strong>Household and machine oils.</strong> Cooking oil, sewing machine oil and
            general-purpose oils are not designed for this surface, can attract dust and grit, and
            can degrade the belt backing.</li>
            <li><strong>Petroleum-based products and grease.</strong> Too thick, and potentially
            harmful to the materials.</li>
            <li><strong>General-purpose silicone sprays.</strong> Many contain solvents or
            propellants alongside the silicone. Treadmill lubricant labelled as 100% silicone is a
            different product even if the word silicone is on both cans.</li>
            <li><strong>Wax, unless the manual names it.</strong> A small number of machines specify
            a wax-based product. If yours does not, do not improvise.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The rule is simple enough to remember: if the bottle does not say 100% silicone and
            treadmill, it does not go under the belt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="steps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Step by Step: How to Lubricate a Walking Pad</h2>
<p>
            This method assumes a typical flat walking pad with the motor at the front and two
            tension bolts at the rear, one on each side, that adjust the rear roller. Some pads put
            the bolts behind small caps in the rear end covers; a few are laid out differently. Where
            your manual describes a different layout or method, follow it.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Unplug and let it cool.</strong> Pull the plug at the wall. If the pad has
            just been used, give the motor a few minutes. Move the pad somewhere you can kneel at the
            back of it with a little space on each side.</li>
            <li><strong>Clear the deck edges.</strong> Wipe the visible edges of the deck and the
            belt sides with a dry cloth. Dust and fibres pushed under the belt with the lubricant end
            up where you do not want them. If it is very dirty underneath, our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-clean-under-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cleaning under a treadmill belt</a>
            covers that first.</li>
            <li><strong>Find the rear tension bolts.</strong> Look at the rear end caps on either
            side of the back roller. There is usually a hex bolt head on each side, sometimes behind
            a small removable cap.</li>
            <li><strong>Mark and count.</strong> Put a dot of marker or a small piece of tape on the
            head of each bolt so you can see its position. Then loosen each bolt anticlockwise by the
            same number of turns — often two or three full turns is enough to give the belt some
            slack — and write the number down. Loosen both sides equally, so the roller stays
            square.</li>
            <li><strong>Lift one edge of the belt at the centre of the deck.</strong> Halfway along
            the pad, lift the belt edge away from the deck. On a loosened belt you should get enough
            of a gap to see the deck surface with a torch. Do not force it; if there is no gap, back
            the bolts off another turn each, and add that to your count.</li>
            <li><strong>Apply silicone under the centre.</strong> Reach the nozzle under the lifted
            edge and apply a thin line of silicone across the deck towards the middle, where your
            feet land, rather than at the very front or back. Use the quantity the manual gives; if it
            gives none, a modest amount is the right instinct — a thin line, not a puddle.</li>
            <li><strong>Repeat on the other side.</strong> Lift the opposite edge at the same point
            and apply a matching line, so both halves of the deck get the same.</li>
            <li><strong>Restore the tension.</strong> Turn each bolt clockwise by exactly the number
            of turns you loosened it, until the marks line up where they started. The belt should be
            back at the tension it had before you began.</li>
            <li><strong>Wipe any excess.</strong> Check the belt edges and the floor for drips and
            wipe them off. Silicone on the top of the belt makes it slippery underfoot.</li>
            <li><strong>Walk it in.</strong> Plug in, start the belt at the lowest speed, and watch it
            run empty for a minute to check it is tracking straight. Then walk on it at a slow pace —
            around 1 to 2 mph — for three to five minutes. That spreads the silicone across the whole
            contact area; until you do, it sits in two stripes and does very little.</li>
            <li><strong>Check tension and centring.</strong> Stop, watch the belt run, and correct
            any drift or slip with small adjustments, as described in the next two sections.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            A note on step four. On some pads there is enough give to lift an edge at the centre
            without touching the bolts at all. If you can see the deck clearly with a gentle lift,
            skip the loosening and you will not have to restore anything. Loosen only if you would
            otherwise be forcing the belt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tension" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. How Do You Get the Belt Tension Right Afterwards?</h2>
<p>
            If you counted the turns and put them back, the tension should be where it was before
            you started, and that is usually correct. Check it before walking on the pad for long.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Signs it is too loose</h3>
          <p>
            The belt hesitates or slips under your foot as you land, especially when you push off,
            and the motor may seem to keep turning while the belt stutters. Tighten both bolts by
            the same small amount — a quarter turn each — and test again. Be aware that a freshly
            lubricated deck can make a marginal belt slip briefly until the silicone has been walked
            in; if a few more minutes of walking cures it, leave the tension alone.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Signs it is too tight</h3>
          <p>
            The belt cannot be lifted at all at the centre, the motor sounds strained, the pad runs
            warmer than usual, or the belt seems to drag. Over-tightening loads the motor, the
            rollers and their bearings, and is one of the quieter ways to shorten a pad's life.
            Loosen both bolts a quarter turn and test again.
          </p>
          <p>
            Manuals describe the right tension in different ways — often as a small amount of lift
            available at the middle of the belt. Follow your manual's description if it has one. Our
            guide to
            <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how tight a treadmill belt should be</a>
            explains the checks in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="centre" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. How Do You Re-Centre the Belt After Lubricating?</h2>
<p>
            Run the pad empty at a low speed and watch the belt. If it stays an even distance from
            both sides of the deck, leave it. If it drifts towards one side, it needs a small
            correction at the rear bolts.
          </p>
          <p>
            The usual rule is to turn the bolt on the side the belt has drifted towards a quarter
            turn clockwise, which nudges the belt back towards the centre. Make one quarter-turn
            adjustment at a time, let the belt run for a minute to settle, and look again. Belts
            respond slowly, and impatient half-turn adjustments are how a belt ends up swinging from
            one side to the other. Some manuals suggest loosening the opposite side instead, which
            keeps the overall tension the same; either works if done in small steps.
          </p>
          <p>
            A belt that will not stay centred however carefully it is adjusted may have uneven
            tension, a worn belt or a roller problem, and is worth raising with the seller if the
            pad is under warranty. For the full method, including how to tell whether drift is
            caused by the way you walk, see our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to centre a treadmill belt</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-often" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. How Often Should You Lubricate a Walking Pad?</h2>
<p>
            As often as the manual says, and if it gives an interval in hours of use rather than in
            months, you will probably reach it sooner than you expect. Walking pads are often used
            for far more hours a week than a treadmill in a spare room, because they run in the
            background of a working day.
          </p>
          <p>
            Manuals vary: some give an interval in hours, some in months, some say to lubricate
            when the deck feels dry. Pre-waxed decks may give a long interval or none. Where a manual
            gives hours, it helps to know how fast you are using them. The table below is
            arithmetic, using an illustrative 50-hour interval — substitute your manual's figure.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">How you use the pad</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Hours a week</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Weeks to reach 50 hours</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Evening walks, a few times a week</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 17</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">One hour every weekday</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Two hours every weekday at a desk</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Three hours every weekday at a desk</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 3 and a half</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Four hours every weekday at a desk</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2 and a half</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The practical point is that a desk walker on a pad can hit an hours-based interval in a
            month or so, while someone using it for evening walks may go a season. A calendar
            reminder set by months alone can leave a heavily used pad running dry for weeks. If
            your pad or app tracks total hours or distance, use that.
          </p>
          <p>
            Heavier users, long continuous sessions and dusty rooms all tend to shorten the
            interval. Over-lubricating is also possible: too much silicone makes the belt slip and
            can work its way out onto the floor or the rollers, so more often is not automatically
            better. For a fuller treatment of intervals on all kinds of treadmill, see our guide to
            <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how often to lubricate a treadmill</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What Are the Signs Your Walking Pad Needs Lubricating?</h2>
<p>
            The clearest sign is a dry deck. With the pad unplugged, lift the belt edge at the middle
            of the deck and run a fingertip across the surface where your feet land. It should feel
            slightly slick. If it feels dry, papery or grippy, it is due. Check in the middle of the
            walking area rather than at the edge, where wear is lighter.
          </p>
          <p>
            Other signs, any of which can point to a dry deck:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The belt hesitates as you land.</strong> A brief slowing with each step, as
            friction between belt and deck rises.</li>
            <li><strong>New noise.</strong> A squeak, a rubbing sound or a motor that sounds more
            laboured than it used to at the same speed.</li>
            <li><strong>The pad runs warmer.</strong> The motor housing at the front is noticeably
            warmer after a normal session than it used to be.</li>
            <li><strong>Cut-outs or error codes.</strong> Some pads protect themselves by stopping or
            showing an error when the motor is overloaded, and a dry deck is one of the commonest
            reasons for overload.</li>
            <li><strong>The belt feels heavier.</strong> If you stop the motor and push the belt by
            hand with the pad unplugged, it drags more than you remember.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A burning smell is different. Stop, unplug, let the pad cool and investigate before
            using it again; lubrication may be part of the fix, but a hot smell is a reason to stop
            rather than to carry on and hope.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="if-something-goes-wrong" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. What If Something Feels Wrong Afterwards?</h2>
<p>
            Most problems after lubricating a pad come from tension, tracking or quantity, and they
            are fixable in a few minutes.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The belt slips</h3>
          <p>
            Walk for a few more minutes first; fresh silicone can make a belt slip briefly until it
            spreads. If the slip persists, the tension may not be fully restored — tighten both
            bolts a quarter turn and test. If it still slips, too much lubricant may have been
            applied; unplug, lift the edges, and wipe the excess from the deck edges with a lint-free
            cloth, then walk it in again.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The belt drifts to one side</h3>
          <p>
            Usually uneven tension from restoring one bolt more than the other. Correct it with
            quarter turns as described above.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The noise has not gone</h3>
          <p>
            If a squeak or rub continues after the silicone has been walked in, the noise may not be
            coming from the deck at all — it may be a roller, the belt edge rubbing on a cover, or
            something loose. Check that the belt is centred and not touching the side covers.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">There is silicone on the floor or belt surface</h3>
          <p>
            Wipe it off. Silicone on the top of the belt is slippery underfoot, and on a hard floor
            it is a slip hazard around the pad. Next time, use less.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">When to stop and contact the seller</h3>
          <p>
            If the pad cuts out repeatedly, smells hot, will not track however it is adjusted, or the
            deck surface looks worn through, stop using it and contact the seller or brand. On a pad
            still under warranty, describe what you did and what happened; following the manual's
            maintenance method is part of what keeps a claim straightforward.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you oil a walking pad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only with 100% silicone treadmill lubricant, unless your manual names another product. Household oils, machine oils, WD-40 and petroleum-based products can damage the deck and belt backing and attract grit. Some walking pads have a pre-waxed deck that should not be lubricated at all, so check the manual before adding anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you have to loosen the belt to lubricate a walking pad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not always. On some pads there is enough give to lift the belt edge at the middle of the deck without touching the bolts. If you would have to force it, loosen both rear bolts by the same counted number of turns, apply the silicone, then restore exactly the same number of turns so the tension and tracking stay as they were.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much lubricant does a walking pad need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use the quantity in the manual. If it gives none, a modest amount is right: a thin line under each side of the belt, towards the middle of the deck. Too much makes the belt slip and can spread onto the floor or rollers. You can always add a little more later; removing excess is more work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where do you put the lubricant on a walking pad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On the deck surface under the belt, towards the middle of the pad where your feet land, applied from each side by lifting the belt edge. Not on top of the belt, not on the rollers and not near the motor at the front. Walking at a low speed for a few minutes afterwards spreads it across the whole contact area.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it bad to never lubricate a walking pad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a pad whose manual calls for lubrication, yes. A dry deck raises friction, which makes the motor work harder, run hotter and wear faster, and wears the belt and deck too. On a pad with a pre-waxed or maintenance-free deck, follow the manual, which may say no lubrication is needed for a long period or at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my walking pad slipping after I lubricated it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fresh silicone can make a belt slip briefly until it is walked in, so walk at a low speed for a few more minutes first. If it continues, the tension may not have been fully restored; tighten both rear bolts a quarter turn. If it still slips, too much lubricant may have been used, and the excess needs wiping from the deck edges.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Lubricating a Walking Pad</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The job is simple. These four mistakes are what turn it into a tracking problem, a slipping belt or a damaged deck.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not counting the turns</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Loosening the rear bolts without counting means guessing when you put the tension back, and a guess usually leaves one side tighter than the other. The belt then drifts or slips. Mark each bolt head, count the turns as you loosen, write the number down, and restore exactly that number on each side.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the wrong product</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">WD-40, household oil and general-purpose silicone sprays are the common culprits. They can strip or soften the deck surface and the belt backing and attract grit, and the damage is not always reversible. Use only 100% silicone treadmill lubricant, or the specific product your manual names.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Lubricating a pre-waxed deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Some pads are designed not to be lubricated. Adding silicone to a pre-waxed or maintenance-free deck can make the belt slip and may complicate a warranty claim. Read the manual before you buy lubricant, and if it says the deck needs nothing, contact the seller about any dry-deck symptoms rather than treating them yourself.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the walk-in</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Silicone applied and left sits in two stripes and does almost nothing until it is spread. Three to five minutes of slow walking distributes it across the area your feet use. Skip it, and the first proper session does the job unevenly while you wonder why the pad still feels dry or briefly slips.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

