        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">To lubricate a walking pad, unplug it, loosen the rear bolts by a
            counted number of turns, apply 100% silicone under the centre of the belt from each side,
            restore the same turns, walk it in slowly and re-centre if needed.</strong> Fifteen
            minutes, and the tools usually came in the box.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Check the manual first, because some pads are pre-waxed and should be left alone, and
            never use WD-40 or household oils. Desk walkers reach hours-based intervals quickly, so
            track hours rather than months. For the general method on full-size machines, see our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill belt lubrication guide</a>.
          </p>
